findings.scenario:
```
@fastmath
fn bad(x: f64) -> bool:
    return x.is_nan()

fn main():
    z = 0.0
    println(bad(z / z))
```
On Linux with gcc or clang, glibc's `isnan` is `__builtin_isnan`, which -ffinite-math-only folds to 0, so the program prints `false`. MSVC's UCRT `isnan` goes through `fpclassify`/`_dclass` and prints `true`: the same program gives different answers on different toolchains. clang 18 and later also warns, by default, on the fastmath unit: `use of NaN is undefined behavior due to the currently enabled floating-point options [-Wnan-infinity-disabled]` (and 'use of infinity' for is_finite/is_infinite), which violates [CG-C-2]. The build does not show it because run() only fails on a non-zero exit. The same mechanism lets `Unit.clamped(nan)` in a @fastmath function become a max/min instruction whose NaN operand order is unspecified. A range value could then hold NaN ([RNG-7]), and an Option[Unit] niche would read Some(nan) as None in strict code.
